{
  "dom_id": "step-q42344",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14260559/assets/05deff2a6188cbeb6bc44711eb803759ba1b85673b3a438dc1b1fd4e39b7f362.png)\n\nThe Venn diagram shown above represents two events $A$ and $B$ and the number of outcomes in each region of the sample space.\n\nGiven that every outcome is equally likely, find $P(A\\cap {B}^{′}).$",
  "worked_solution": "To calculate the probability of an event using a Venn diagram, we count the number of outcomes in the region that represents the event, and divide by the total number of outcomes of the sample space $S.$\n\nIn the Venn diagram, $A\\cap {B}^{′}$ is represented by the area inside the circle $A$ without the intersection between the circles (see the illustration below).\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14260559/assets/0a3e69dae369e382cf80a2fa2d2c7012310c30f38704f17d3219ded12f06b7d7.png)\n\nWe count up the outcomes:\n\n- The number of outcomes in $A\\cap {B}^{′}$ is $32.$\n\n- The total number of outcomes in $S$ is\n\n$$\n32+22+42+50=146.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(A\\cap {B}^{′})=\\frac{32}{146}=\\frac{16}{73}.\n$$",
